    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> This Beautiful Day</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> It's Raining! I Need an Umbrella.</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Describe simple personal experiences and habits. Interactive: Participate in simple dialogues on familiar topics.</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe simple personal experiences with the weather (e.g., "I ride a bike on sunny days!"). Can participate in simple dialogues about the weather (e.g., "What's the weather today?" "It's rainy."). Can describe the weather and their feelings (e.g., "I am happy on sunny days.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e6eef5; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e6eef5; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e6eef5; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and starts a quick "Weather Check" using large weather flashcards (sun, rain, cloud, wind). The teacher shows a flashcard and asks, "What's the weather like?" Students respond chorally. Then, the teacher models describing feelings: "It's sunny. I'm happy!" and "It's rainy. I'm sad." The teacher then points to different students and asks, "How do you feel today?" to elicit simple personal responses (e.g., "I'm good," "I'm happy").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher explicitly models sentences for describing weather and feelings, focusing on contractions and present simple. For example, the teacher points to the "sunny" flashcard and says clearly, "It's sunny today. I'm happy on sunny days!" The teacher over-enunciates the /s/ sound in "sunny." Then, the teacher points to the "rainy" flashcard, makes a sad face, and says, "It's rainy. I'm bored on rainy days." The teacher models the /r/ sound in "rainy" and blends /r-eɪn/ for "rain."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of weather and feeling vocabulary and target structures.
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; padding-left: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a 'sunny' flashcard) "Is it rainy?" (Expected answer: "No, it's sunny!")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher points to a happy student) "Are you sad?" (Expected answer: "No, I'm happy!")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "This Beautiful Day".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated dialogue between two 3rd-grade children, Sofia and Leo, talking about the weather and their feelings/activities. The dialogue is displayed on the board or as a handout with clear pictures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Dialogue Example:</b><br/>
                    <b>Sofia:</b> Hi Leo! How do you feel today?<br/>
                    <b>Leo:</b> I feel happy, thank you! It's sunny.<br/>
                    <b>Sofia:</b> Yes, it's bright and warm. I love sunny days. I play outside!<br/>
                    <b>Leo:</b> Me too! What about rainy days?<br/>
                    <b>Sofia:</b> When it's rainy, I'm a little sad. I stay inside and read books. I need an umbrella!<br/>
                    <b>Leo:</b> Oh, I enjoy rainy days sometimes. I watch movies. But I need my boots if I go out.
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Participates in simple oral exchanges related to familiar topics and immediate needs.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher reads the dialogue aloud twice, modeling pronunciation. Students listen and follow along.
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; padding-left: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What are Sofia and Leo talking about?" (Expected: "Weather and feelings.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b>
                            <ul style="list-style-type: circle; margin-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; padding-left: 0;">
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Leo feels sad today." (False)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Sofia likes to play outside on sunny days." (True)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Sofia needs a coat when it's rainy." (False, she needs an umbrella)</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using contractions ("I'm," "it's"), WH-questions ("How do you feel today?", "What's the weather like?"), present simple for habits ("I play," "I read"), and target vocabulary (sunny, rainy, happy, sad, bored, umbrella, boots). Focus on pronunciation of /s/ in "sunny" and /r/ in "raining" and blending phonemes (/r-eɪn/, /ˈr-eɪn-i/).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; padding-left: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Sentence Completion (Oral):</b> The teacher displays sentence starters on the board or using flashcards. Students complete them orally, chorally, and individually.
                            <ul style="list-style-type: circle; margin-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; padding-left: 0;">
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"It's _____. I feel _____." (e.g., "It's cloudy. I feel bored.")</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When it's sunny, I _____." (e.g., "When it's sunny, I play.")</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When it's rainy, I need a(n) _____." (e.g., "When it's rainy, I need an umbrella.")</li>
                            </ul>
                            The teacher specifically prompts for contractions ("It's," "I'm") and monitors /s/ and /r/ pronunciation.
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Substitution Drill (Pair Work):</b> Students work in pairs. Partner A asks, "What's the weather like today?" Partner B looks at a weather picture card (e.g., windy, hot, cold) and responds, "It's _____. I feel _____." Then Partner A asks, "What do you do when it's _____?" Partner B responds, "I _____." They swap roles after 3-4 rounds. Teacher provides a list of verbs (play, read, watch, sleep, draw) and adjectives (happy, sad, bored, excited, lazy) to help. Teacher reminds students to use polite phrases ("thank you").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Guided Pair Interaction - Q&A:</b> Students stay in pairs. The teacher provides sentence frames for a mini-dialogue on the board:
                            <ul style="list-style-type: circle; margin-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; padding-left: 0;">
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A: "Hi [Name]! How do you feel today?"</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">B: "I'm [feeling], thank you. It's [weather]."</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A: "What do you do when it's [weather]?"</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">B: "I [activity]."</li>
                            </ul>
                            Students practice these dialogues with different weather and feeling words. Teacher circulates, providing feedback on pronunciation, grammar, and vocabulary use.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "This Beautiful Day".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Students work in pairs to create and perform a short, simple dialogue (role-play) based on the "This Beautiful Day" scenario and "It's Raining! I Need an Umbrella" theme. Each pair will perform their dialogue for another pair or the class.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; padding-left: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reminds students of the dialogue from Stage 2 and the sentence frames from Stage 3.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">In pairs, students choose two different weather conditions (e.g., sunny and rainy, or windy and cloudy) and discuss what they do and how they feel during each. They should also mention if they need an item (umbrella, coat, boots).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students create a simple 4-6 line dialogue incorporating:
                            <ul style="list-style-type: circle; margin-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; padding-left: 0;">
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Asking "How do you feel?"</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Describing the weather ("It's sunny," "It's rainy").</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Expressing feelings ("I'm happy," "I'm sad").</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Describing simple habits ("I play," "I read").</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Expressing a need ("I need an umbrella," "I need my boots").</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students practice their dialogue several times, focusing on clear pronunciation of /s/ and /r/, using contractions, and polite expressions.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pairs then perform their dialogues for another pair or the whole class.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the pair performances. The teacher will listen for specific language points from the Learning Outcome and Competences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform their created dialogues for the class or another pair.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; padding-left: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear articulation of words, especially /s/ in "sunny" and /r/ in "raining", and correct blending of phonemes.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Includes at least 3-4 weather words, 2-3 feeling words, and 1-2 items (umbrella, boots, coat).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Fluency:</b> Speaks in short, understandable sentences, using contractions ("It's," "I'm") and present simple.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration/Interaction:</b> Participates actively and appropriately in the dialogue, using polite phrases.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper before leaving:
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; padding-left: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new weather word did you learn or use today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What's one thing you can say about how you feel about the weather?" (e.g., "I'm happy on sunny days.")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-8 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; padding-left: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to talk about the weather and your feelings today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What do you want to practice more next time?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students were able to use the target vocabulary and contractions in simple sentences. The pair work and guided dialogues were effective for building confidence in speaking. Some students still need more practice with the /r/ sound and sentence formation for activities ("I play" vs. "I playing"). For the next lesson, I need to incorporate more opportunities for sentence construction related to personal experiences, which will transition well into writing. The exit tickets showed good understanding of key vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This speaking lesson provided ample practice in forming sentences about weather, feelings, and activities. The next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing) will build on these oral sentences, guiding students to write simple descriptive sentences and short paragraphs about their experiences with different types of weather, using the same vocabulary and grammar structures.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3 min</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>